course: world
lesson: 102
title: Quarter past, quarter to, and how people really say the time
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An hour is like a pizza cut into 4 equal slices: each slice is a quarter of an hour, 15 minutes, because 60 ÷ 4 = 15.
explain: "Past" means after the hour and "to" means before the next one. Quarter past eight is 8:15; quarter to seven is 6:45, fifteen minutes before seven, not after it. Once the long hand passes half past, people switch to "to": twenty to ten is 9:40.
explain: The same clock is said differently in different places. In parts of the USA "quarter of five" or "quarter till five" means 4:45 and "ten after five" means 5:10. In Britain "half three" means 3:30, but German "halb drei" means half way to three, 2:30. Same-sounding words, an hour apart!
explain: Some time words are fuzzy or old: a Scottish gran's "the back of nine" means just after nine, and an old book's "five and twenty past four" is 4:25, because old English could say the small number first.
explain: When someone uses a time word you don't know, ask for the digital time: being right matters more than sounding clever.
fact: A quarter of an hour is 15 minutes, because 60 ÷ 4 = 15.
fact: Quarter past means 15 minutes after the hour, so quarter past eight is 8:15.
fact: Quarter to means 15 minutes before the hour, so quarter to seven is 6:45.
fact: In parts of the USA "quarter of" or "quarter till" means quarter to, so "quarter of five" is 4:45.
fact: Americans often say "after" where British people say "past": ten after five and ten past five are both 5:10.
fact: In German "halb drei" means half way to three, 2:30, while in British English "half three" means 3:30.
fact: In Australia "arvo" means afternoon, so "half three this arvo" is 3:30 in the afternoon.
fact: Old books sometimes put the small number first: "five and twenty" is 25 and "four and twenty" is 24.
fact: To turn "twenty to ten" into digital, take 20 from 60 to get 40 and use the hour before: 9:40.
fact: In parts of northern England and Scotland "dinner" can mean the midday meal and "tea" the evening meal, so "dinner's at half twelve" means 12:30 in the day.
fact: In Scotland "the back of nine" means a little after nine, around 9:05 or 9:10, never nearly ten.
fact: 7:45 can be said "seven forty-five" or "quarter to eight"; "three quarters past seven" is also true, because 3 × 15 = 45, but almost nobody says it.
fact: Words people use: "mam" means mum (Ireland, the north of England, Wales), "faffing" means wasting time on little things, "muppet" means a silly person, "howay" means "come on" (north-east England) and "yinz" means "you all" (Pittsburgh, USA).
exercise: How many minutes are in a quarter of an hour? | 15 | 25 | 4 | 30
exercise: Oisin's mam shouts, "Bus is at quarter past eight!" It is 8:00 now. How long has he got? | 15 minutes | 25 minutes | 45 minutes | 8 minutes
exercise: In Ohio, Tyler says practice is at "quarter of five". What time does he mean? | 4:45 | 5:15 | 4:15 | 5:45
exercise: Tyler's English cousin turns up at 5:15 for practice at "quarter of five". How late is he? | 30 minutes | 15 minutes | 45 minutes | He is right on time
exercise: Chloe's dad says the film starts at "quarter to seven". When is that? | 6:45 | 7:15 | 7:45 | 6:15
exercise: Lily's German friend says the party is at "halb drei". When does it start? | 2:30 | 3:30 | 3:00 | 2:00
exercise: Sean's Aussie uncle texts, "come round this arvo, bout half three". When should Sean go? | 3:30 in the afternoon | 3:30 in the morning | 2:30 in the afternoon | 3:00 in the evening
exercise: Grace reads "it was five and twenty past four" in an old book. What time is it? | 4:25 | 4:52 | 5:20 | 4:05
exercise: The coach says, "We kick off at twenty to ten." What is that in digital? | 9:40 | 10:20 | 9:20 | 10:40
exercise: Hannah's mum in the north of England says "dinner's at half twelve". When do they eat? | 12:30 in the middle of the day | 6:00 in the evening | 12:30 at night | 6:30 in the evening
exercise: Kyle's gran in Scotland says, "Be in by the back of nine." Roughly when should he be home? | A little after nine | Just before ten | Exactly at half past nine | Before eight o'clock
exercise: Jake says "ten after five". What does his British friend call the same time? | Ten past five | Ten to five | Five past ten | Ten to six
exercise: Which is NOT a usual way to say 7:45? | Three quarters past eight | Quarter to eight | Seven forty-five | Fifteen minutes to eight
exercise: Why do people switch from "past" to "to" after half past? | The next hour is now closer, so they count down to it | The clock changes direction after half past | Minutes after 30 do not count as minutes | It is a law to say it that way
exercise: How do you turn "twenty to" into digital minutes? | Take 20 away from 60 | Add 20 to 60 | Use 20 as the minutes | Take 20 away from 30
test: Chloe goes, "Quarter to seven? Easy, that's quarter past, so 7:15." What's the right answer? | 6:45, because "to" means before seven | 7:15, because quarter always means after | 7:45, because "to" means three quarters | 6:15, because quarter to means a quarter of six
test: Lily turned up at 3:30 to her German friend's "halb drei" party. How long had it been going? | 1 hour | 30 minutes | It hadn't started yet | 2 hours
test: Sean's mum reads "come round this arvo" and asks, "Is that the morning?" What does "arvo" mean? | Afternoon | Morning | Tomorrow | Evening
test: Kyle comes home at 9:45 and says, "That's the back of nine, innit?" Is he right? | No, the back of nine is just after nine, so he is well late | Yes, the back of nine means any time before ten | Yes, it means quarter to ten exactly | No, the back of nine means before nine
test: Hannah's London friend turns up at 6 in the evening for "dinner at half twelve". What went wrong? | In the north "dinner" can be the midday meal, at 12:30 | The friend should have come at half past six | Half twelve means six o'clock in London | Northerners eat dinner at midnight
test: Jake's mate laughs, "It's ten PAST, ya muppet, not ten after." Who is right? | Both are right; British say "past", Americans often say "after" | Only the mate; "after" is not a real time word | Only Jake; "past" is old-fashioned | Neither; the time is five to ten
test: Mateo rolls in at 9:50 for a twenty-to-ten kick-off, saying, "Howay man, I'm early!" Is he? | No, kick-off was at 9:40, so he's 10 minutes late | Yes, kick-off is at 10:20 | Yes, by exactly 10 minutes | No, but only by 1 minute
test: Dev says "three quarters past seven" and the class laughs. Is he wrong about the time? | No, it is 7:45, just said in an unusual way | Yes, three quarters past seven is 7:34 | Yes, it is 8:45 | Yes, it is 7:15
test: Tyler's Pittsburgh aunt says, "Yinz gotta be there at quarter till three." When must they arrive? | 2:45 | 3:15 | 3:45 | 2:15
test: What would happen if you went to every "quarter of" meeting in the USA at quarter past instead? | You would always be 30 minutes late | You would always be 15 minutes early | You would always be exactly on time | You would always be an hour late
test: Oisin's mam yells, "Stop faffing!" at 8:00, with the bus at quarter past eight. What does she want? | Stop wasting time, the bus goes in 15 minutes | Stop eating, the bus came 15 minutes ago | Go back to bed, the bus is at quarter to nine | Stop running, the bus waits 45 minutes
exam: Determine the number of minutes comprised in one quarter of an hour. | Fifteen | Twenty-five | Four | Thirty
exam: It is given that an appointment is fixed for "quarter to seven". Ascertain the corresponding time in digital notation. | 6:45 | 7:15 | 7:45 | 6:15
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the German expression "halb drei". | It denotes half past two | It denotes half past three | It denotes three o'clock precisely | It denotes a quarter past three
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the American expression "quarter of five"? | Fifteen minutes before five o'clock | Fifteen minutes after five o'clock | One quarter of five minutes | Five minutes after a quarter past the hour
